        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"center\":1,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":4,\"age_fit\":3,\"continuity\":4,\"delight\":3},\"evidence\":{\"center\":\"Cruz acts in nearly every chapter, but Phoenix is the worst-covered child and appears by name only in Chapter 1 (\\\"Uh oh,\\\" \\\"What are you making?\\\", pulling the duck). From Chapter 2 onward, lines that should be his go to Cruz: \\\"said Cruz... said Cruz\\\", \\\"Cruz grabbed Cruz's hand\\\", \\\"Cruz carried planks. Cruz handed out nails.\\\" and \\\"said Cruz, with a big grin.\\\" Phoenix is active in 1 of 10 chapters, which scores 1. The rule capping a child silent for three or more consecutive chapters also applies. No likes were stated, so there is no likes deduction.\",\"craft\":\"The shape is strong: a cold open on a wobbling tower at home, a glowing-brick device, a problem (bricks that don't bind) that builds to the tubes breakthrough, and a return home with the tower finally going \\\"CLICK. And it held.\\\" Chapters 8 and 9 are thin montage stops with no real beat beyond pointing and gasping.\",\"facts\":\"The key facts are accurate: Billund, 1932 toys, leg godt and 1934, the rebuilt workshop, the motto, the 1947 moulding machine, the 28 January 1958 patent, Kjeld as tester and later head, LEGOLAND 1968, and the minifigure 1978. Two small blurs: the True Facts list dates the fire to 1942 but the story never gives a date, and \\\"Automatic Binding Bricks\\\" (named 1949) is placed in 1947. Having Cruz invent the tubes is a fictional liberty that the story never marks as pretend.\",\"age_fit\":\"Length (1538 words) and the short, repetitive, sound-word style suit Band A, and dates and the patent give the 7-year-old hooks. However, Chapter 3 brings in a night fire: \\\"FIRE! The workshop is on fire!\\\", flames in the dark and the children running outside. Band A allows no peril, even though this is resolved within the chapter.\",\"continuity\":\"This is the first story, so there is no bible to contradict. There is some internal inconsistency: Phoenix pressed the brick and both boys were pulled \\\"into\\\" it, yet it later glows \\\"in Cruz's pocket\\\". The duplicated-name lines, such as Cruz holding Cruz's hand, also muddle who is who.\",\"delight\":\"There are lovely read-aloud beats: \\\"quack-clack\\\", \\\"CLUNK. HISSSS. POP!\\\", the held-it-held-it rhythm, and the \\\"Play well\\\" goodnight. But a parent will stumble aloud on \\\"Cruz grabbed Cruz's hand\\\" and on back-to-back \\\"said Cruz\\\" lines, and a listening Phoenix will notice he has vanished.\"},\"caps_applied\":[\"none\"],\"disqualified\":false,\"overall\":3.1,\"editor_notes\":[\"Restore Phoenix throughout. Give him the second line in each duplicated pair (\\\"Duck Company?\\\" said Phoenix; Phoenix grabbed Cruz's hand; Phoenix handed out nails; the second brick test; the minifigure walk; the closing \\\"Kids like us\\\"), and have Kjeld invite both boys by name.\",\"Soften the fire for Band A. Tell it the morning after, with the boys finding Ole among the ashes and helping rebuild, instead of waking to shouting and flames in the dark.\",\"Give Chapters 8 and 9 a hands-on beat each. For example, one boy spots a tiny model of something from home at LEGOLAND, and the other bends the minifigure's legs to sit it in the car they built with Kjeld. Also note lightly that the tubes idea is make-believe, since Godtfred's team designed them.\"],\"best_moment\":\"He pulled. It held. He shook. It held. He turned it upside down and shook it really hard. It still held!\",\"worst_moment\":\"Orange flames danced in the dark. Cruz grabbed Cruz's hand, and they ran outside with everyone else.\"}"
